---
/**
 * Where the traditional material on this site comes from.
 *
 * Every reference page separates "traditional view" from "modern view" from
 * "Mo's synthesis". This page names the texts and lineages behind those labels
 * so readers can distinguish inherited technique from later interpretation and
 * from Mo's own synthesis.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import PageJumpLinks from '../../components/PageJumpLinks.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Sources', href: '/explore/sources/' },
];
const sourceRoutes = [
  { label: 'Where “traditional” comes from', href: '#traditional-sources', note: 'Hellenistic, Persian, Arabic, medieval, Renaissance, and horary lineages.' },
  { label: 'Where “modern” comes from', href: '#modern-practice', note: 'Later psychological, humanistic, and outer-planet-era developments.' },
  { label: 'What “Mo’s synthesis” means', href: '#mos-synthesis', note: 'Where studied technique ends and Mo’s own interpretation begins.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sources, where the traditional material comes from"
  description="Classical and modern texts that inform the traditional material on Mo Lumen Astrology, with distinctions among inherited technique, later practice, and Mo's own synthesis."
  path="/explore/sources/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Where this comes from</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Every reference page here separates traditional material, modern practice, and my own
          synthesis. This page names some of the texts and lineages behind those distinctions so
          you can see what is inherited, what developed later, and where my interpretation begins.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.src}
        srcset={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.alt}
        width={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.width}
        height={ocean.cliffsSeaFog.height}
        style="transform: scaleX(-1)"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>Use this page as a map, not a reading list</h2>
      <p>
        When a reference page says <em>traditional view</em>, <em>modern view</em>, or <em>Mo's
        synthesis</em>, those labels are doing different jobs. You do not need to read every source
        below to use the site. This index is here so you can see where a claim belongs and follow it
        further when you want to.
      </p>
      <PageJumpLinks heading="Which layer are you trying to trace?" items={sourceRoutes} />

      <h2 id="traditional-sources">Why I name the traditional sources</h2>
      <p>
        When I say <em>traditional view</em>, I want that phrase to point somewhere specific. The
        techniques did not arrive as one seamless system, and astrologers across periods did not
        always agree. Naming the source makes it easier to see which ideas belong to which strand
        of the tradition and to follow them further if you want to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Older does not automatically mean truer. Traditional and modern approaches differ in
        important places, and the reference pages try to make those differences visible rather
        than quietly choosing one vocabulary and presenting it as universal.
      </p>

      <h2>The Hellenistic foundation</h2>
      <p>
        Much of the technical vocabulary later used in Western astrology can be found in texts from
        the Greek-speaking Mediterranean of the Hellenistic and late-antique periods. These are
        among the works most often referenced when this site discusses older doctrine and technique.
      </p>
      <div class="source-index">
        <article>
          <h3>Dorotheus of Sidon</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Carmen Astrologicum</em> · 1st century CE</p>
          <p>Early instructional material including triplicity rulers and timing techniques.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Claudius Ptolemy</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Tetrabiblos</em> · 2nd century CE</p>
          <p>Influential later Western source for planetary condition and rulership doctrine.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Vettius Valens</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Anthology</em> · 2nd century CE</p>
          <p>A large collection of techniques and chart examples showing astrology in practice.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Firmicus Maternus</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Mathesis</em> · 4th century CE</p>
          <p>A substantial Latin compendium preserving another strand of late-antique practice.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>Persian, Arabic, and medieval transmission</h2>
      <div class="source-index">
        <article>
          <h3>Abu Ma'shar</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">9th century</p>
          <p>An influential astrologer in the transmission of astrological material through Arabic and later Latin intellectual traditions.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Guido Bonatti</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Liber Astronomiae</em> · 13th century</p>
          <p>A major medieval synthesis frequently referenced in horary and electional technique.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>The English horary tradition</h2>
      <p>This is one strand in which I have formal training, so it is especially relevant to how I describe my own background.</p>
      <article class="source-feature">
        <h3>William Lilly</h3>
        <p class="card__meta"><em>Christian Astrology</em> · 1647</p>
        <p>
          An important English-language source for horary practice. My Practitioner's Certificate
          in Horary Astrology is from the
          <a href="https://www.sta.co/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">School of Traditional Astrology</a>,
          which teaches within this lineage.
        </p>
      </article>

      <h2>Modern recovery and scholarship</h2>
      <p>
        Many older astrological texts became much easier for English-speaking practitioners to
        study during the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries through new translations,
        editions, and historical work. That recovery helped make older techniques much more visible
        in contemporary practice.
      </p>
      <div class="source-index">
        <article>
          <h3>Project Hindsight</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">From the 1990s</p>
          <p>Translation and recovery work associated with figures including Robert Hand, Robert Schmidt, and Robert Zoller.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Chris Brennan</h3>
          <p class="card__meta"><em>Hellenistic Astrology: The Study of Fate and Fortune</em> · 2017</p>
          <p>A modern synthesis of historical sources and the revival of Hellenistic practice.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Deborah Houlding</h3>
          <p class="card__meta">Contemporary scholarship and teaching</p>
          <p>Widely referenced work on houses, traditional symbolism, and horary practice.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.keys} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2 id="modern-practice">Where modern practice develops differently</h2>
      <p>
        When a page here says <em>modern view</em>, it usually points to approaches that developed
        well after the older texts above, including the twentieth-century psychological and
        humanistic strands associated with writers such as Alan Leo and Dane Rudhyar.
      </p>
      <p>
        Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto also entered astrological practice after their astronomical
        discoveries, so their modern meanings and rulerships do not come from the ancient or
        medieval sources. The reference pages label those distinctions rather than folding every
        period into one tradition.
      </p>

      <h2 id="mos-synthesis">And when it's just me</h2>
      <p>
        <em>Mo's synthesis</em> means my own reading: the way I put studied techniques, chart
        context, observation, and the question in front of me together. I label it separately so
        you can tell the difference between a source tradition and my interpretation of it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Keep following the ideas"
    body="Explore Astrology teaches the vocabulary and techniques. Current Sky is where I use that language to look at changing cycles in real time."
    buttonLabel="Explore Astrology"
    buttonHref="/explore/"
    secondaryLabel="See Current Sky"
    secondaryHref="/current-sky/"
  />

  <style>
    .source-index { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: var(--space-4); margin-block: var(--space-5) var(--space-8); }
    .source-index article, .source-feature { padding: var(--space-5); border: 1px solid var(--border-color, currentColor); border-radius: var(--radius-lg, 1rem); }
    .source-index h3, .source-index p:last-child, .source-feature h3, .source-feature p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
    @media (min-width: 768px) { .source-index { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
